 "caveats": {
  "canonical_oracle_sample": "Oracle-selected corners on the canonical sets exist only where all three incident edges have a strict line in the top-5, which is 0.45% of eval56 corners and 0.46% of wood corners.  That is 6 and 5 corners across three seeds.  The canonical oracle rates (41.7% and 0.0%) are therefore not usable numbers and are reported only for completeness; the finding rests on availability, not on them.",
  "selection_gain_is_real_but_unqualified": "S1 topology-consistent selection lifts synthetic corners from 15.5% to 39.5% within 20px, +24pp over S0, and the oracle reaches 97.1%.  So a selection component genuinely exists.  The protocol does not award SELECTION_FAILURE because its precondition (J1 or J2 hypothesis presence) failed: with triplet availability at 19.6% against a 60% gate, a better selector cannot reach candidates that are absent for 80% of corners.",
  "endpoints_are_not_the_bottleneck": "L1_INFINITE_LINE_ONLY is 1.2% of synthetic available edges and 6.1% on eval56, and top-5 strict segment (60.1%) sits within 1.3pp of top-5 strict infinite line (61.3%).  Once the infinite line is right the extent is almost always right too, so an endpoint representation would not have fixed this.",
  "diagnostic_only": "Oracle selection uses ground truth to pick among the top-5 and is an upper bound, never a method.  Finite PnP is not accurate pose."
